      "blind_working": "A metal with low ionisation enthalpy forms its cation easily. This favours ionic bonding, along with a negative electron gain enthalpy and a high lattice enthalpy."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u03_t02_2",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        4
      ],
      "blind_answer": 4,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "ΔHf = 100 + 500 + 240/2 − 350 − 800 = −430 kJ/mol."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u03_t02_3",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        1
      ],
      "blind_answer": 1,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Ionisation of Na (+496) outweighs electron gain by Cl (−349). The large lattice enthalpy released when the gaseous ions pack into the crystal makes the overall process exothermic."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u03_t02_4",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        3
      ],
      "blind_answer": 3,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "A is true: net ≈ +147 kJ/mol. R is false: the ionisation enthalpy of Na exceeds the magnitude of Cl's electron gain enthalpy."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u03_t02_5",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        4
      ],
      "blind_answer": 4,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "I is incorrect: octet attainment alone is not enough, and lattice enthalpy is needed. II is correct: the Born-Haber cycle applies Hess's law to find lattice enthalpy indirectly."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u03_t03_1",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        3
      ],
      "blind_answer": 3,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "By definition (NCERT), lattice enthalpy is the energy required to completely separate one mole of a solid ionic compound into its gaseous ions."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u03_t03_2",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        3
      ],
      "blind_answer": 3,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "MgO and CaO have 2+/2- ions, so they come first; Mg2+ is smaller than Ca2+, so MgO > CaO. Among the 1+/1- salts, F- is smaller than Cl-, so NaF > NaCl. Order: MgO > CaO > NaF > NaCl."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u03_t03_3",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        3
      ],
      "blind_answer": 3,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Ratio = (2x2/200) / (1x1/300) = (4 x 300)/200 = 6."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u03_t03_4",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        1
      ],
      "blind_answer": 1,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "NaCl has lattice enthalpy +788 kJ/mol. LiF has the smallest ions, so it is the highest among alkali halides. CsI has the largest ions, so it is the lowest. Lattice enthalpy is determined via the Born-Haber cycle."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u03_t03_5",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        4
      ],
      "blind_answer": 4,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "A is false: lattice enthalpy decreases as interionic distance increases. B is true: higher lattice enthalpy means higher melting point and hardness. C is true: a salt dissolves if hydration enthalpy compensates lattice enthalpy. D is false: it cannot be measured directly and is found indirectly via Born-Haber. E is true: it is proportional to the product of ionic charges. Correct set: B, C, E."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u03_t04_1",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        4
      ],
      "blind_answer": 4,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Fluorine has the highest Pauling electronegativity, about 4.0. It is higher than O (3.44), Cl (3.16) and N (3.04)."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u03_t04_2",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        2
      ],
      "blind_answer": 2,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Pauling relation (kcal/mol): Δ = 23(Δχ)². So (Δχ)² = 25/23 = 1.087, giving Δχ ≈ 1.04."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u03_t04_3",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        2
      ],
      "blind_answer": 2,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Carbon is sp in ethyne (50% s), sp2 in ethene (33%) and sp3 in ethane (25%). More s-character makes carbon more electronegative, so ethyne > ethene > ethane."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u03_t04_4",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        4
      ],
      "blind_answer": 4,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "A is false: the energy released on adding an electron to an isolated gaseous atom is electron gain enthalpy, not electronegativity. R is true: electronegativity is a relative, unitless property of a bonded atom."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u03_t04_5",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        2
      ],
      "blind_answer": 2,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Statement I is incorrect: oxygen is second, but its Pauling value is about 3.44–3.5, not 3.0. Statement II is incorrect: Δχ for C–H is about 0.35, while for H–Cl it is about 0.96, so H–Cl is more polar."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u03_t05_1",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        4
      ],
      "blind_answer": 4,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "With the same Li+ cation, the largest anion is the most polarisable. I- is largest, so LiI has the maximum covalent character."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u03_t05_2",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        2
      ],
      "blind_answer": 2,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Cation charge rises and size falls from Na+ to Mg2+ to Al3+. Polarising power rises in that order, so AlCl3 > MgCl2 > NaCl."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u03_t05_3",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        1
      ],
      "blind_answer": 1,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Cu+ has an 18-electron configuration (3d10). The d electrons shield the nucleus poorly, so Cu+ is more polarising than Na+ with its noble gas configuration."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u03_t05_4",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        4
      ],
      "blind_answer": 4,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "A: Li+ is smaller than Na+, same charge, both noble gas type (II). B: Cu+ has an 18-electron configuration (III). C: I- is larger and more polarisable than F- (I). D: Al3+ carries a higher charge (IV)."
    },
    {
