      ],
      "blind_answer": 2,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "A pi orbital has a nodal plane that contains the internuclear axis. The pi* orbital also has an extra nodal plane between the nuclei. The sigma orbitals have no nodal plane containing the axis."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u03_t12_3",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        2
      ],
      "blind_answer": 2,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Because of overlap and repulsion effects, the antibonding MO is raised in energy slightly more than the bonding MO is lowered."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u03_t12_4",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        2
      ],
      "blind_answer": 2,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "A is true: an electron in sigma*1s cancels bonding stabilisation. R is true: sigma*1s is cylindrically symmetric about the axis. However, this symmetry does not explain destabilisation; the node between the nuclei does."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u03_t12_5",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        2
      ],
      "blind_answer": 2,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Statement I is false: a sigma orbital has no nodal plane containing the internuclear axis. Statement II is false: bonding electrons concentrate density between the nuclei, which reduces repulsion and stabilises the molecule."
    },
